# 992-android-persona-mobile-behaviour.patch
#
# camchrome: an Android persona (--fingerprint-platform=android) answered like a phone (UA,
# UA-CH mobile, portrait screen, coarse pointer, touch points, mobile web prefs from 002)
# but did not behave like one. This closes the three behavioural gaps:
#
# (A) Mouse becomes touch. RenderWidgetHostImpl::Init() turns on the TouchEmulator for the
#     main frame widget with the GENERIC_MOBILE gesture config -- the path DevTools device
#     mode uses for Emulation.setEmitTouchEventsForMouse. Pages see touchstart/touchend,
#     pointerType "touch" and tap-synthesised clicks; CDP Input.dispatchMouseEvent goes
#     through the same emulator, so automation clicks become taps too. emulation_handler.cc
#     re-enables it instead of disabling it when a DevTools client turns its own touch
#     emulation off or detaches.
#
# (B) A phone held in a hand. On a machine with no orientation/motion sensors the Android
#     persona used to fire one deviceorientation event with null angles and devicemotion
#     with null vectors -- a desktop's answer. held_device_sensor.{h,cc} models a portrait
#     handset tilted 35-65 degrees towards the face (posture from the persona seed) with arm
#     sway, physiological tremor and per-sample noise, quantised the way the sensor service
#     rounds real readings (0.1 deg, 0.1 m/s^2, 0.1 deg/s). Gravity, rotation rate and the
#     angles derive from one motion so they agree. A connected (or DevTools-overridden)
#     sensor still wins; the model only fills the no-reading case.
#
# (C) Handset fonts. font_cache.cc gets an "android" platform list: the named families and
#     aliases in AOSP 13/14 /system/etc/fonts.xml (sans-serif-*, serif, monospace, casual,
#     cursive, Roboto, Noto Serif, Droid Sans Mono, Cutive Mono, Coming Soon, Dancing Script,
#     Carrois Gothic SC, and the Arial/Helvetica/Tahoma/Verdana/Times/Georgia/... aliases).
#     The Noto script and emoji fallbacks have no name and do not match by family on a phone,
#     so they are not listed. Desktop-only "basic" families (Helvetica Neue, Sans,
#     BlinkMacSystemFont) no longer pass through for an Android persona. Each handset family
#     renders through a metric clone (Arimo/Tinos/Cousine/Comic Neue/Liberation Sans Narrow).
#
# Known interaction: CDP Input.dispatchTouchEvent (e.g. Playwright's tap()) switches the tab's
# emulator to injected-touch mode; mouse events sent after it reach that page as mouse events
# until its main-frame widget is recreated. Drive an Android persona with mouse events or the
# Browser.humanized* commands, which all become touch, rather than mixing in raw touch events.
#
# (D) Touch is switched on per tab at the first mouse event that reaches web content
# (http/https/file/data/blob/about:blank); browser UI pages (chrome://, DevTools, extensions)
# keep the mouse. CDP Input.dispatchMouseEvent returns once the emulator has turned the event
# into touch (stock Chrome never answers it while the emulator is on).
#
# (E) Fonts: on a macOS host the handset families render through macOS system fonts (Helvetica
# Neue, Arial Narrow, Menlo, Courier New, Times New Roman, Georgia, Comic Sans MS); the Linux
# metric clones are not installed there.
#
# (F) getVoices() lists the Google TTS locale set the way Chrome on Android names it
# ("English United States", lang en_US, first voice default), and outerWidth is the screen width.
#
# Not covered: the Generic Sensor API classes (Accelerometer, Gyroscope) still report no
# sensor; Android DRM robustness levels; the layout viewport is the desktop window's, so launch
# the window phone-sized (--window-size) for innerWidth to match the persona screen.
#
# Tests: tools/fingerprint_selftest.py --platform android checks touch feature detection,
# that a CDP mouse click arrives as touchstart + pointerdown:touch, streaming orientation,
# gravity magnitude and the handset font set; desktop personas check a click stays a mouse.
